<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seven Sages of Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th–6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seven Sages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> were statesmen and philosophers of the 7th and 6th centuries BCE honoured in Greek tradition for their wisdom and maxims. Lists varied, but usually included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales of Miletus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bias-of-priene">Bias of Priene</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittacus">Pittacus of Mytilene</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleobulus">Cleobulus</a> of Lindos. Sayings such as &quot;know thyself&quot; were attributed to them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seven-sages-of-greece.jpg" alt="Seven Sages of Greece" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clemens Schmillen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NMBSevenSagesMosaicBaalbekRectified.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
